    "innovation": "This paper demonstrates that a decadal prediction initialized in 2013, based on off-equatorial Pacific Ocean heat content, successfully simulates a transition to the positive phase of the Interdecadal Pacific Oscillation and a consequent resumption of accelerated global warming.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Interdecadal Pacific Oscillation (IPO)",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The negative phase of the Interdecadal Pacific Oscillation (IPO), a dominant mode of multi-decadal variability of sea surface temperatures (SSTs) in the Pacific, contributed to the reduced rate of global surface temperature warming in the early 2000s.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "El Ni\u00f1o/Southern Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "tropical Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "A proposed mechanism for IPO multidecadal variability indicates that the presence of decadal timescale upper ocean heat content in the off-equatorial western tropical Pacific can provide conditions for an interannual El Ni\u00f1o/Southern Oscillation event to trigger a transition of tropical Pacific SSTs to the opposite IPO phase.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Pacific subtropical cells (STCs)",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The mechanism, as originally formulated, has been independently confirmed and generalized in the context of the subtropical gyre and the Pacific subtropical cells (STCs)12.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "El Ni\u00f1o",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "eastern equatorial Pacific (5\u00b0S\u20135\u00b0N, 160\u00b0W-80\u00b0W)",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The 1972-73 El Ni\u00f1o, evidenced by an increase in vertically integrated heat content to the depth of the 18\u00b0C isotherm in the eastern equatorial Pacific (5\u00b0S\u20135\u00b0N, 160\u00b0W-80\u00b0W) to values near 1.5 \u00d7 1024 J (Fig. 1b) is followed by a subsequent decrease over the next few years in off-equatorial southwestern tropical Pacific heat content by nearly 0.1 \u00d7 1024 J (Fig. 1a).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "El Ni\u00f1o",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "eastern equatorial Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "However, the subsequent 1976\u201377 El Ni\u00f1o, with an increase in vertically integrated heat content in the eastern equatorial Pacific of ~ 1.4 \u00d7 1024 J (Fig. 1b), is associated with a subsequent drop in off-equatorial northwestern and southwestern tropical Pacific heat content for the next decade and longer (Fig. 1a).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "La Ni\u00f1a",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "eastern equatorial Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "These low heat content values in the off-equatorial western Pacific in the ocean initial states were then associated with the 2-year La Ni\u00f1a event (1998\u20132000) with low eastern equatorial Pacific heat content values down to near 1.0 \u00d7 1024 J (Fig. 1b) and a transition to the negative phase of the IPO.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CCSM4",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "Pattern correlation",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "Figure 2 shows hindcast verification in terms of pattern correla- tion for Pacific SSTs (Pacific basin, ocean grid points 40\u00b0S-70\u00b0N, 100\u00b0E-80\u00b0W) for CCSM4 for the ensemble average of ten ensemble members for each initial year starting in 1960 (that is, the correlation is calculated using the ensemble average\u00b9).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "El Ni\u00f1o",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "eastern equatorial Pacific (5\u00b0N-5\u00b0S, 170\u00b0W-120\u00b0W)",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The ensemble average for the Ni\u00f1o3.4 index in the eastern equatorial Pacific (5\u00b0N-5\u00b0S, 170\u00b0W-120\u00b0W, a standard ENSO index) tracks the observations for the onset of the El Ni\u00f1o in mid-1976 and then the ensemble average remains anomalously warm through 1978, in fact longer than what was observed (Fig. 3a).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "La Ni\u00f1a",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Ni\u00f1o3.4 area (5\u00b0S-5\u00b0N, 120\u00b0W-170\u00b0W)",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Of more significance for the IPO transition, the ensemble average model prediction shows below-normal Ni\u00f1o3.4 SSTs for the observed 2-year La Ni\u00f1a from 1998 to 2000, with most ensemble members having negative values for that time period in qualitative agreement with the observations. This La Ni\u00f1a simulation was associated with the prediction of the transition of the IPO from positive to negative in the model for the 3- to 7-year predictions initialized in 1996 (ref. 1).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Interdecadal Pacific Oscillation (IPO)",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "eastern tropical Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The associated SST anomalies for the 3- to 7-year prediction (2015\u20132019 minus a 15-year average before the initial year, 1998\u20132012, used as a reference climatology with which to compare the initialized prediction) initialized in 2013 show a positive phase of the IPO, with above-normal SSTs predicted over the eastern tropical Pacific extending into the northeast Pacific, with low amplitude-negative SST anomalies in the northwest and southwest Pacific (Fig. 4a).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Interdecadal Pacific Oscillation (IPO)",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "northeast Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The associated SST anomalies for the 3- to 7-year prediction (2015\u20132019 minus a 15-year average before the initial year, 1998\u20132012, used as a reference climatology with which to compare the initialized prediction) initialized in 2013 show a positive phase of the IPO, with above-normal SSTs predicted over the eastern tropical Pacific extending into the northeast Pacific, with low amplitude-negative SST anomalies in the northwest and southwest Pacific (Fig. 4a).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Interdecadal Pacific Oscillation (IPO)",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "northwest and southwest Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The associated SST anomalies for the 3- to 7-year prediction (2015\u20132019 minus a 15-year average before the initial year, 1998\u20132012, used as a reference climatology with which to compare the initialized prediction) initialized in 2013 show a positive phase of the IPO, with above-normal SSTs predicted over the eastern tropical Pacific extending into the northeast Pacific, with low amplitude-negative SST anomalies in the northwest and southwest Pacific (Fig. 4a).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Pacific Decadal Oscillation (PDO)",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Other studies have shown less skill for the smaller North Pacific area defined for the Pacific Decadal Oscillation (PDO)27. Although the PDO and IPO are closely related28, the smaller area strictly defined for the PDO in the North Pacific has more spatial noise and less predictive skill for that small area than the much larger Pacific-wide SST pattern defined for the IPO.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CCSM4",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SIMULATION_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "initialized",
            "target_entity_type": "SimulationType",
            "paragraph": "The 3- to 7-year hindcasts with CCSM4 initialized every year show skill in simulating patterns of SST in the Pacific associated with the IPO, except for periods when there are disruptions from volcanic eruptions with a post-eruption sequence of Pacific SST patterns that do not agree with the ensemble average forced response in the model23.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CCSM4",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SIMULATION_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "forced ocean-sea ice simulation",
            "target_entity_type": "SimulationType",
            "paragraph": "As described in a previous study21, the hindcasts in CCSM4 are initialized using a forced ocean-sea ice simulation designed to reproduce the evolution of the ocean and sea ice states from the start of 1948 through the end of 2013.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
